 income tax). Bars scaled relative to the highest-take-home state. Effective tax rate = total tax ÷ gross salary.</p></div></div><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-12\"><section><h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4\">$175,000<!-- --> After Tax in 2026 — Overview</h2><p class=\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4\">On a <!-- -->$175,000<!-- --> annual salary in 2026, your federal income tax is approximately<!-- --> <strong class=\"text-foreground\">$30,734</strong> for a single filer claiming the standard deduction of $16,100. FICA payroll taxes (Social Security + Medicare) add another <strong class=\"text-foreground\">$13,388</strong>. The big variable is state income tax — your take-home pay swings by more than<!-- --> <strong class=\"text-foreground\">$12,356</strong> per year depending on where you live.</p><p class=\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\">In Texas and Florida (no state income tax), you keep the most of your <!-- -->$175,000<!-- --> paycheck at around <strong class=\"text-foreground\">$130,879</strong> per year — that&#x27;s about <strong class=\"text-foreground\">$10,907</strong> per month. In California, your net pay drops to roughly <strong class=\"text-foreground\">$118,522</strong> <!-- -->(<!-- -->$9,877<!-- -->/month) after state income tax of up to 13.3% kicks in. Illinois sits in the middle with its flat 4.95% rate, leaving you with about<!-- --> <strong class=\"text-foreground\">$122,353</strong> annually.</p></section><section><h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4\">Top 10 States by Take-Home Pay on <!-- -->$175,000</h2><p class=\"text-muted-foreground mb-4\">These ten states give you the highest net pay on a <!-- -->$175,000<!-- --> salary. Notice that the top of the list is dominated by states with no income tax — Texas, Florida, Washington, Nevada, and others. Even within no-tax states, take-home pay is identical because the only deductions are federal tax and FICA.</p><div class=\"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border/30\"><table class=\"w-full text-sm\"><thead class=\"bg-muted/30\"><tr><th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-foreground\">Rank</th><th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-foreground\">State</th><th class=\"text-right p-3 font-semibold text-foreground\">Net Annual</th><th class=\"text-right p-3 font-semibold text-foreground\">Monthly</th><th class=\"text-right p-3 font-semibold text-foreground\">Bi-Weekly</th><th class=\"text-right p-3 font-semibold text-foreground\">Eff. Tax Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"border-t border-border/20\"><td class=\"p-3 text-muted-foreground\">1</td><td class=\"p-3 font-medium text-foreground\">Texas</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-foreground\">$130,879</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">$10,907</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">$5,034</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">0.3<!-- -->%</td></tr><tr class=\"border-t border-border/20\"><td class=\"p-3 text-muted-foreground\">2</td><td class=\"p-3 font-medium text-foreground\">Florida</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-foreground\">$130,879</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">$10,907</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">$5,034</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">0.3<!-- -->%</td></tr><tr class=\"border-t border-border/20\"><td class=\"p-3 text-muted-foreground\">3</td><td class=\"p-3 font-medium text-foreground\">Washington</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-foreground\">$130,879</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">$10,907</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">$5,034</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-muted-foreground\">0.3<!-- -->%</td></tr><tr class=\"border-t border-border/20\"><td class=\"p-3 text-muted-foreground\">4</td><td class=\"p-3 font-medium text-foreground\">Nevada</td><td class=\"p-3 text-right text-foreground\">$130,879</td><td class=\"p-