eground\",\"children\":\"$$30,734\"}],\" for a single filer claiming the standard deduction of $16,100. FICA payroll taxes (Social Security + Medicare) add another \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"className\":\"text-foreground\",\"children\":\"$$13,388\"}],\". The big variable is state income tax — your take-home pay swings by more than\",\" \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"className\":\"text-foreground\",\"children\":\"$$12,356\"}],\" per year depending on where you live.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"In Texas and Florida (no state income tax), you keep the most of your \",\"$$175,000\",\" paycheck at around \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"className\":\"text-foreground\",\"children\":\"$$130,879\"}],\" per year — that's about \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"className\":\"text-foreground\",\"children\":\"$$10,907\"}],\" per month. In California, your net pay drops to roughly \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"className\":\"text-foreground\",\"children\":\"$$118,522\"}],\" \",\"(\",\"$$9,877\",\"/month) after state income tax of up to 13.3% kicks in. Illinois sits in the middle with its flat 4.95% rate, leaving you with about\",\" \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"className\":\"text-foreground\",\"children\":\"$$122,353\"}],\" annually.\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4\",\"children\":[\"Top 10 States by Take-Home Pay on \",\"$$175,000\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground mb-4\",\"children\":[\"These ten states give you the highest net pay on a \",\"$$175,000\",\" salary. Notice that the top of the list is dominated by states with no income tax — Texas, Florida, Washington, Nevada, and others. Even within no-tax states, take-home pay is identical because the only deductions are federal tax and FICA.\"]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border/30\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"